Dear Chris

Thank you so much for the update and for following up so quickly. It is
great to hear that you were able to use the photos from our website.

Regarding your request, unfortunately, the photos posted on our website are
the highest resolution files we have available. The physical photos
themselves still belong to the original donor families and institutions, so
we are unable to rescan these with higher resolution. We hope you
understand our constraints.

I would also like to correct my earlier email for some of the credit lines,
particularly for the ones taken in Aukang. I sincerely apologise for the
oversight.

Please amend the following crediting lines for photos 7-9 in my previous
email. These should be attributed to the *Mary and Joseph Lee Collection*:

   1. Working life in the kampong =E2=80=93 a young fisherman mending the p=
rawn
   trawler nets, c. 1930s. Mary and Joseph Lee Collection, courtesy of the
   Chancery Archives.
   2. Kampong girls riding around the Ponggol 10th milestone neighbourhood,
   c. 1930s. Mary and Joseph Lee Collection, courtesy of the Chancery
   Archives.
   3. The extended Chng Family comprising three generations of Aukang
   Teochew Catholics in front of an attap house, 1932. Mary and Joseph Lee
   Collection, courtesy of the Chancery Archives.

Thank you and we wish you all the best in the continuation of your website
and Youtube channel.

God Bless,
Amanda Lim
*Records & Archives Executive*
